// @run
// @log ok
// @log ok
// @hint redundant type annotation: `named` is inferred as `{f: () => string}` — remove it to lean on inference
// D976 — A CLOSURE-VALUED FIELD CALLED THROUGH AN UN-ANNOTATED PARAM. `vl check` returned 0
// and the module did not load: the call lowered correctly and `print` picked
// `__print_i32__` for a `(ref $string)`.
//
// The decline was the LAST guard in `exprString`'s Member arm. `fieldClosureFeOfRecv`
// resolves the field's `fe` and `fRetKind[fe]` is already `"str"` — both verified at the
// print site itself, keyed on the argument node rather than on the property name, which is
// what three earlier probes got wrong. But the arm then defers to the checker's recorded
// result type, and through a hole param that type still carries a TYPE VARIABLE, so
// `tyKindOf` answered something that is not 3 and the string claim was dropped.
//
// That deferral exists to catch a real twin-layout hazard: a `{f: (…) => string}` field and a
// `{f: (…) => boolean}` twin collapse to the same layout heap type, so the fe can resolve the
// WRONG lambda. A RESOLVED disagreement must still win — that check is untouched. A HOLE is
// not a disagreement, it is the absence of an opinion, so it no longer overrides the fe.
//
// Named and anonymous receivers both, because the shape's nominality was never the axis. The
// annotation on `named` is DECLARED redundant rather than removed: it is what makes this the
// named-type case, and the axis was checked precisely because the two behave alike.
//
// ONE receiver shape per module here, deliberately. A module carrying a string-returning
// closure field AND a non-string one of the same layout is broken on master too — before
// this fix it did not build at all — and is filed as D987. Folding it in would make this
// fixture grade that instead of this.
type S = { f: () => string }

const named: S = { f: () => "ok" }

const anon = { f: () => "ok" }

function callNamed(x) { print(x.f()) }

function callAnon(y) { print(y.f()) }

callNamed(named)

callAnon(anon)
